>> title: Ching Bo Leung Soup
>> category: Soups
>> tags: pork, chinese yam, lotus seeds, lily bulb, job's tears, cantonese, old-fire soup, summer, stovetop
>> counters: The Soup Pot
>> aka: 清補涼湯, cing bou loeng tong, ching bo leung tong, ching bo leung soup, ching po leung, chinese herbal soup mix, 老火湯, lo fo tong
>> servings: 6
>> time: 3 hr 30 min
>> slack: forgiving — nothing in the packet can overcook and an extra hour only deepens it; what will not come back is a lotus seed with its green core left in, which turns the whole pot bitter
>> step.1: Summer, and the name says what it is for: 清補涼 — clearing and supplementing without heating. This is the one soup on the shelf that is about the dried goods rather than about a vegetable, so every one of them carries its own reason here. It is bought as a mixed packet and the contents vary by shop and by season.
>> step.2: blanch from cold, then rinse the ribs
>> step.3: soak the packet 30 min
>> step.4: simmer 3 hr, barely a quiver
>> step.5: season at the end, never at the start
>> step.6: The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.

Summer, and the name says what it is for: 清補涼 — clearing and supplementing without heating. This is the one soup on the shelf that is about the dried goods rather than about a vegetable, so every one of them carries its own reason here. It is bought as a mixed packet and the contents vary by shop and by season.

Blanch @pork ribs{1 1/2%lb}(680 g; cut into single ribs) from cold in @water{2%qt}(1.9 L) in a #soup pot{}, ~blanch{10%min} up to a rolling boil, then pour the whole lot away, rinse the ribs under the tap and scrub the pot out.

Soak @dried Chinese yam{1%oz}(30 g; waai saan — 健脾, the spleen-and-stomach word), @Solomon's seal{1/2%oz}(15 g; juk zuk — for a dry throat, and half of a pair), @adenophora root{1/2%oz}(15 g; saa sam — the other half of that pair; the two are sold and used together), @lotus seeds{1/3%cup}(lin zi; the bitter green core pushed out of each one, or the pot turns), @dried lily bulb{1/4%cup}(baak hap; 安神, the calm-and-sleep word), @fox nut{1/4%cup}(hin sat; a fixture of the mixed packet) and @job's tears{1/4%cup}(ji mai; 祛濕 — the damp word, and the reason this is a summer pot) together in cold water, ~soak{30%min}, then lift them out and leave the grit behind.

Simmer @&(~2)blanched ribs{} with @&(~1)soaked packet{} and @cold water{3 1/2%qt}(3.3 L; the pot is filled once and not topped up) in the rinsed #soup pot{}, lid ajar, ~simmer{3%hr} at a bare quiver, unstirred.

Season @&(~1)soup{} with @kosher salt{1%tsp}, at the end and never at the start.

The broth is the dish. The solids — 湯渣, tong zaa — are spent by the end; they go on a side plate with soy sauce, or they do not.
